sec.p:
Learning-related variables were derived from an ideal observer model, providing a principled and consistent reference across subjects and studies. The ideal observer model was implemented with a well-known solution based on a Hidden Markov Model (2; 54). In short, the model provides optimal estimates of the changing quantity of interest (item probability or reward level) θt given the observations so far, y1:t. This is achieved by iteratively combining the likelihood of the current observation and a prior:


p(θt+1∣y1:t+1,v)∝p(yt+1∣θt+1)∫p(θt∣y1:t,v)×p(θt+1∣θt,v)dθt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered}p\left(\theta_{t+1} \mid y_{1: t+1}, v\right) \propto p\left(y_{t+1} \mid \theta_{t+1}\right) \int p\left(\theta_{t} \mid y_{1: t}, v\right) \\ \times p\left(\theta_{t+1} \mid \theta_{t}, v\right) d \theta_{t}\end{gathered}$$\end{document}

The learner assumes that θ can change unexpectedly with some prior probability υ, as captured by p(θt+1 θt, υ), in which case it is resampled uniformly. The volatility υ, the transition matrix and prior probability or reward levels (when applicable) correspond to the generative structure of the task. The prediction of the next stimulus is the average of the posterior distribution:


p(yt+1∣y1:t,v)=∬p(yt+1∣θt+1)p(θt+1∣θt)×p(θt∣y1:t,v)dθt+1dθt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} p\left(y_{t+1} \mid y_{1: t}, v\right)= & \iint p\left(y_{t+1} \mid \theta_{t+1}\right) p\left(\theta_{t+1} \mid \theta_{t}\right) \\ & \times p\left(\theta_{t} \mid y_{1: t}, v\right) d \theta_{t+1} d \theta_{t}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

We formalized the confidence about the prediction for the next stimulus as the log precision of the distribution in the probability learning task


−log⁡(var(θt+1∣y1:t,v))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle -\log \left(\operatorname{var}\left(\theta_{t+1} \mid y_{1: t}, v\right)\right)$$\end{document}



and as the maximum a posteriori probability in the decision task (this difference does not matter in practice). We quantified surprise as log improbability of the observation (28):


−log2⁡(p(yt+1∣y1:t,v))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle -\log _{2}\left(p\left(y_{t+1} \mid y_{1: t}, v\right)\right)$$\end{document}
